# Fragment: the two workers' kernels only — execution-worker and
# compile-worker — layered over common.config and diskless.config, in that order
# (see default.nix).
#
# common.config turns cgroups off for every role, because no supervisor there
# confines a child with them. For these two that stops being true: each one's
# supervisor holds its disposable children — a round each, or a compile each —
# to their memory with the kernel's memory controller: all of them together to
# one total, and each one alone to a max. A child past its own max, or the one
# the kernel picks when the total is reached, is killed with everything in its
# group. The supervisor builds that tree at boot and refuses to start without it
# (engine-supervisor, `Cgroups::create`).
#
# This turns on the cgroup core and one controller, memory, and no other. The
# core comes whole — cgroup v1 hierarchies, cgroup namespaces, the v2 freezer
# and kill — but a child reaches none of it: it runs under an identity of its
# own with no capability, its syscall allowlist has no mount, unshare or setns,
# it may create threads and no process, and every cgroup file is root's to
# write.
#
# Over 6.12 with kconfig this changes five symbols on the diskless kernel:
# CGROUPS, MEMCG, PAGE_COUNTER and SLAB_OBJ_EXT (both selected by MEMCG), and
# SCHED_MM_CID, a promptless default under CGROUPS that only clearing RSEQ
# would remove.

# The cgroup core and its memory controller. Kernel memory — page tables, slab
# objects, thread stacks — is charged together with user memory. There is no
# symbol to leave it out; `cgroup.memory=nokmem` on the command line would, and
# the measured command line does not carry it. That is what charges a child's
# page tables and threads to the child.
CONFIG_CGROUPS=y
CONFIG_MEMCG=y
# The controller's v1 interface. The supervisor speaks v2 only; the v1 files
# are code nothing here reads.
# CONFIG_MEMCG_V1 is not set
# Process counts are held per child by RLIMIT_NPROC under an identity of its
# own (engine-supervisor), not by a controller.
# CONFIG_CGROUP_PIDS is not set
# Every other controller. defconfig turns them on and CGROUPS would bring them
# back: none is used, and each is kernel code a child could reach. Named one by
# one because an omitted name is a controller back in the measured image —
# CGROUP_DEBUG returns through EXPERT (which forces DEBUG_KERNEL), CGROUP_PERF
# through PERF_EVENTS (which cannot be cleared), the network classifiers
# through NET_SCHED, and CGROUP_NET_CLASSID is written as =y. BLK_CGROUP has
# nothing to attach to without BLOCK, and CGROUP_HUGETLB nothing without
# HUGETLBFS; both are named so this reads the same on any kernel it is layered
# on.
# CONFIG_CGROUP_SCHED is not set
# CONFIG_CGROUP_RDMA is not set
# CONFIG_CGROUP_FREEZER is not set
# CONFIG_CGROUP_HUGETLB is not set
# CONFIG_CPUSETS is not set
# CONFIG_CGROUP_DEVICE is not set
# CONFIG_CGROUP_CPUACCT is not set
# CONFIG_CGROUP_PERF is not set
# CONFIG_CGROUP_BPF is not set
# CONFIG_CGROUP_MISC is not set
# CONFIG_CGROUP_DEBUG is not set
# CONFIG_BLK_CGROUP is not set
# CONFIG_CGROUP_NET_PRIO is not set
# CONFIG_NET_CLS_CGROUP is not set
# CONFIG_CGROUP_NET_CLASSID is not set
